rg';\n```\n\n**1. `SELECT *`**\n- It pulls every column from both tables.\n- Both tables probably have an `id` column, so the names collide, and many clients keep only one of them.\n- It also fetches data you don't need, such as password hashes or other PII from `users`.\n- List the columns you actually use.\n\n**2. `lower(u.email)` stops index use**\n- Wrapping the column in a function means a normal index on `users.email` can't be used, so the database scans the whole table.\n- Pick one of these fixes:\n  - Add an expression index: `CREATE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));`. The query then stays as it is.\n  - Store emails already lowercased and compare `u.email = 'a@example.org'` directly.\n  - On Postgres, use a `citext` column.\n\n**3. Formatting**\n- Put each clause on its own line so it's easier to read and review.\n\n**4. Hard-coded email**\n- If the application builds this string, use a bind parameter (`$1` or `?`). That avoids SQL injection and lets the database reuse the query plan.\n\n**5. No ordering or limit**\n- If you only need recent orders, add `ORDER BY o.created_at DESC` and a `LIMIT`.\n- Make sure `orders.user_id` is indexed. It usually is if it's a foreign key, but Postgres doesn't create that index automatically.\n\nA revised version:\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id AS order_id,\n       o.created_at,\n       o.total,\n       u.id AS user_id,\n       u.email\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = lower($1)   -- backed by an index on lower(email)\nORDER BY o.created_at DESC;\n```\n\nThe column names (`created_at`, `total`) are guesses, because I don't have your schema. Tell me which database you use and which columns you need, and I can tailor it. I haven't changed the file.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}